WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.574 --> 00:00:08.364
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.364 --> 00:00:10.214
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.214 --> 00:00:13.084
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.094 --> 00:00:14.784
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.784 --> 00:00:17.284
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:19.304
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.304 --> 00:00:21.084
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.524 --> 00:00:24.624
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's

00:00:24.644 --> 00:00:27.284
part two of our three-part mini-series

00:00:27.554 --> 00:00:29.924
on Nelson and Lady Hamilton.

00:00:30.684 --> 00:00:33.124
In part one, we followed Horatio

00:00:33.124 --> 00:00:35.884
Nelson from a Norfolk parsonage

00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:37.984
to the Battle of the Nile, losing

00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:40.244
an eye and an arm along the way.

00:00:40.814 --> 00:00:43.404
In the rest of the mini-series, today's

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:46.514
episode and the next one, we turn to

00:00:46.514 --> 00:00:49.154
the woman he loved, Emma Hamilton.

00:00:49.774 --> 00:00:52.964
It's an amazing story of adaptation,

00:00:53.184 --> 00:00:56.744
love, desire, cunning, and how one

00:00:56.744 --> 00:00:59.634
woman rose from nothing to become

00:00:59.644 --> 00:01:01.584
the most famous woman in Europe.

00:01:02.344 --> 00:01:04.674
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:04.684 --> 00:01:07.134
minute and get right into it.

00:01:09.244 --> 00:01:12.984
It is the autumn of 1785, and a

00:01:12.994 --> 00:01:15.374
letter is making its way slowly

00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:18.914
across Europe, from London to Naples.

00:01:19.764 --> 00:01:22.194
It is addressed to Sir William

00:01:22.194 --> 00:01:24.504
Hamilton, the British ambassador

00:01:24.771 --> 00:01:26.544
to the Kingdom of Naples.

00:01:27.114 --> 00:01:30.664
The sender is his nephew, a well-born

00:01:30.684 --> 00:01:33.704
young Englishman called Charles Greville.

00:01:34.724 --> 00:01:37.364
The letter is long, polite,

00:01:37.794 --> 00:01:39.764
and very carefully worded.

00:01:40.514 --> 00:01:43.004
And underneath all the politeness,

00:01:43.504 --> 00:01:45.084
is a business proposal.

00:01:46.264 --> 00:01:48.434
Greville has something he

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:50.084
would like to send his uncle.

00:01:51.284 --> 00:01:53.154
He describes the merchandise

00:01:53.524 --> 00:01:54.894
in glowing terms.

00:01:55.304 --> 00:01:57.924
Delivery can be arranged in the spring,

00:01:58.214 --> 00:02:00.954
and he is confident that his uncle will

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.564
be delighted once the goods arrive.

00:02:05.194 --> 00:02:07.054
The goods in question are

00:02:07.054 --> 00:02:09.204
not a painting, or a case of

00:02:09.204 --> 00:02:11.574
wine, or a thoroughbred horse.

00:02:11.834 --> 00:02:14.404
They are a twenty-year-old woman.

00:02:15.494 --> 00:02:17.364
Her name is Emma.

00:02:17.894 --> 00:02:20.854
And at that moment, in a small house

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:23.364
on the edge of London, she is going

00:02:23.374 --> 00:02:26.044
about her life with no idea at all

00:02:26.324 --> 00:02:28.904
that her future is being negotiated, by

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:32.294
post, between her lover and his uncle.

00:02:33.254 --> 00:02:35.544
So, how does a human being

00:02:35.824 --> 00:02:38.384
end up as goods to be traded

00:02:38.454 --> 00:02:40.574
between two English gentlemen?

00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:43.024
To answer that, we need

00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:44.584
to go back twenty years.

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:50.064
"Emma" was born Amy Lyon, on the 26th

00:02:50.064 --> 00:02:54.414
of April, 1765, in a small village in

00:02:54.454 --> 00:02:56.824
Cheshire, in the northwest of England.

00:02:58.004 --> 00:03:01.214
Her father was a blacksmith, but he

00:03:01.224 --> 00:03:03.694
died when she was two months old.

00:03:05.164 --> 00:03:07.364
Her mother found herself suddenly

00:03:07.374 --> 00:03:09.684
a widow with a baby to look after,

00:03:09.924 --> 00:03:11.854
which was not a great position to

00:03:11.854 --> 00:03:14.174
be in in eighteenth-century England.

00:03:15.554 --> 00:03:17.424
She took Emma to live with her

00:03:17.434 --> 00:03:20.684
grandmother in a village in north Wales.

00:03:21.194 --> 00:03:23.074
There was very little money.

00:03:23.284 --> 00:03:24.504
There was no school.

00:03:25.084 --> 00:03:27.424
The young girl never got a formal

00:03:27.464 --> 00:03:30.744
education, and her spelling would remain

00:03:30.794 --> 00:03:33.854
famously chaotic for the rest of her life.

00:03:35.074 --> 00:03:37.584
At twelve years old, she was sent

00:03:37.604 --> 00:03:41.004
out to work as a maid, cleaning and

00:03:41.014 --> 00:03:42.864
looking after other people's children.

00:03:43.784 --> 00:03:47.414
And at fourteen, she moved to London.

00:03:48.464 --> 00:03:52.654
Now, the London of the late 1770s was a

00:03:52.884 --> 00:03:55.794
dangerous place for a poor teenage girl

00:03:56.154 --> 00:03:58.934
with no family connections and no money.

00:03:59.674 --> 00:04:03.644
There were semi-respectable jobs, as maids

00:04:03.644 --> 00:04:06.514
and shop girls, that paid almost nothing.

00:04:07.424 --> 00:04:10.994
And there was another world, of theatres,

00:04:11.374 --> 00:04:13.744
pleasure gardens and wealthy men.

00:04:14.914 --> 00:04:18.624
Emma existed in both, but naturally

00:04:18.804 --> 00:04:20.904
gravitated towards the latter.

00:04:22.664 --> 00:04:24.724
She was, by all accounts,

00:04:25.004 --> 00:04:26.384
stunningly beautiful.

00:04:26.784 --> 00:04:28.754
But not just beautiful in a

00:04:29.054 --> 00:04:31.194
traditional, good-looking way;

00:04:31.354 --> 00:04:34.584
she was mesmerising, striking.

00:04:36.174 --> 00:04:38.054
She found work at a theatre,

00:04:38.354 --> 00:04:39.694
looking after the actresses.

00:04:40.374 --> 00:04:43.534
And, the story goes, she posed as

00:04:43.534 --> 00:04:46.064
the "Goddess of Health" at a strange

00:04:46.074 --> 00:04:49.274
establishment run by a showman who claimed

00:04:49.274 --> 00:04:52.264
electricity could cure all illnesses.

00:04:54.024 --> 00:04:56.934
The details of these years are a little

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:59.974
murky, and some of the stories were

00:04:59.994 --> 00:05:02.684
probably invented later by her enemies.

00:05:03.634 --> 00:05:07.004
But what is certain is that by the

00:05:07.034 --> 00:05:10.954
age of fifteen, Emma had been noticed.

00:05:12.384 --> 00:05:14.734
The man who noticed her was Sir

00:05:14.734 --> 00:05:17.514
Harry Fetherstonhaugh, who was a

00:05:17.524 --> 00:05:20.074
rich young aristocrat with a large

00:05:20.114 --> 00:05:22.248
country estate called Uppark.

00:05:23.284 --> 00:05:25.154
He installed her there as a

00:05:25.154 --> 00:05:27.754
kind of entertainer and hostess

00:05:27.944 --> 00:05:29.284
for his hunting parties.

00:05:30.174 --> 00:05:32.624
One story has her dancing on his

00:05:32.624 --> 00:05:35.714
dining room table naked for his guests.

00:05:37.154 --> 00:05:40.174
She was fifteen years old, surrounded by

00:05:40.174 --> 00:05:44.134
men twice her age, and entirely dependent

00:05:44.254 --> 00:05:46.314
on her host's continued interest.

00:05:47.424 --> 00:05:49.814
Within a year, she was pregnant.

00:05:50.874 --> 00:05:53.644
And Sir Harry did what rich men

00:05:53.654 --> 00:05:55.604
in his position generally did

00:05:55.814 --> 00:05:57.484
in the eighteenth century when a

00:05:57.544 --> 00:05:59.324
teenage mistress became pregnant.

00:05:59.904 --> 00:06:01.466
He abandoned her.

00:06:02.821 --> 00:06:05.511
Emma, in her desperation, wrote

00:06:05.561 --> 00:06:07.521
to another man she had met at

00:06:07.561 --> 00:06:11.101
Uppark, a quieter, more calculating

00:06:11.121 --> 00:06:13.271
aristocrat called Charles Greville.

00:06:14.021 --> 00:06:16.061
She begged him for help.

00:06:17.491 --> 00:06:19.651
Greville agreed to take her

00:06:19.651 --> 00:06:22.411
in, but he had conditions.

00:06:23.401 --> 00:06:26.001
The baby, a girl, would be sent

00:06:26.001 --> 00:06:27.941
away, to be raised by Emma's

00:06:27.951 --> 00:06:30.111
relatives and later by strangers.

00:06:31.451 --> 00:06:34.401
And Emma would get a new, settled

00:06:34.431 --> 00:06:37.481
identity: from now on, she was

00:06:37.491 --> 00:06:39.921
to be plain "Mrs Emma Hart".

00:06:40.631 --> 00:06:43.371
The "Mrs" was pure fiction; it was

00:06:43.381 --> 00:06:45.611
the polite way of keeping a mistress.

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:49.769
She would live quietly in a small house

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:52.449
on the edge of London, see nobody from

00:06:52.449 --> 00:06:55.849
her old life, and belong to him alone.

00:06:57.359 --> 00:07:00.109
She was sixteen, and with no

00:07:00.149 --> 00:07:02.269
other options, or at least no

00:07:02.269 --> 00:07:04.529
other good options, she agreed.

00:07:05.666 --> 00:07:09.966
Now, it's not exactly clear why Greville

00:07:09.966 --> 00:07:13.516
took her in, but one hypothesis is that

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:15.676
he thought he could make money from her.

00:07:16.676 --> 00:07:19.426
Specifically, as an artist's model.

00:07:20.866 --> 00:07:23.576
She had this stunning look, remember,

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:26.136
and people wanted portraits of

00:07:26.216 --> 00:07:27.596
beautiful people on their wall.

00:07:28.636 --> 00:07:30.606
He took her to be painted by one

00:07:30.626 --> 00:07:32.646
of the most fashionable portrait

00:07:32.646 --> 00:07:34.916
painters in London, George Romney.

00:07:36.166 --> 00:07:38.246
Whether or not Greville ever made

00:07:38.246 --> 00:07:41.026
any money from it is unclear, but

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:43.476
what certainly did happen is that

00:07:43.486 --> 00:07:45.996
George Romney, the portrait painter,

00:07:46.286 --> 00:07:49.176
became utterly obsessed with Emma.

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:56.056
Between 1782 and 1786, Emma sat

00:07:56.066 --> 00:07:58.946
for him more than a hundred times.

00:07:59.936 --> 00:08:02.976
He painted her as goddesses, as saints,

00:08:03.246 --> 00:08:07.076
as characters from mythology: as Circe the

00:08:07.076 --> 00:08:10.476
enchantress, as Cassandra, as Joan of Arc.

00:08:11.816 --> 00:08:14.306
And Emma was far more than

00:08:14.306 --> 00:08:15.856
a pretty face to be copied.

00:08:16.196 --> 00:08:18.336
She had a natural gift for

00:08:18.366 --> 00:08:20.366
performance, she could hold an

00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:22.816
emotion for hours on end, which

00:08:22.816 --> 00:08:25.186
made her an ideal artist's model.

00:08:26.546 --> 00:08:28.646
Prints of the paintings were copied

00:08:28.666 --> 00:08:31.036
and sold across the country, and

00:08:31.366 --> 00:08:33.713
lo and behold, the blacksmith's

00:08:33.753 --> 00:08:35.763
daughter  became one of the most

00:08:35.763 --> 00:08:37.753
recognisable faces in Britain.

00:08:38.993 --> 00:08:42.243
She was, in effect, the eighteenth-century

00:08:42.243 --> 00:08:45.353
version of a supermodel, a woman whose

00:08:45.353 --> 00:08:47.883
face everyone knew, attached to a

00:08:47.883 --> 00:08:50.633
person almost nobody had actually met.

00:08:52.163 --> 00:08:54.963
But to Greville, Emma

00:08:55.223 --> 00:08:56.906
had become a problem.

00:08:58.353 --> 00:09:01.503
He had expensive tastes and not

00:09:01.573 --> 00:09:03.273
enough money to sustain them.

00:09:04.253 --> 00:09:06.013
The solution, he decided,

00:09:06.283 --> 00:09:08.463
was to marry a rich heiress.

00:09:09.613 --> 00:09:12.533
But no respectable heiress would

00:09:12.533 --> 00:09:14.343
marry a man who openly kept a

00:09:14.353 --> 00:09:17.443
famous young mistress, so this meant

00:09:17.683 --> 00:09:20.183
finding a way of getting rid of Emma.

00:09:21.293 --> 00:09:23.963
So Greville came up with a plan,

00:09:24.413 --> 00:09:26.803
and this is where the letter from

00:09:26.803 --> 00:09:28.723
the start of this episode comes in.

00:09:30.083 --> 00:09:32.733
His uncle was Sir William Hamilton,

00:09:33.043 --> 00:09:35.063
the British ambassador in Naples.

00:09:35.893 --> 00:09:38.943
At the time, Naples was the capital of

00:09:38.943 --> 00:09:41.503
its own country, the Kingdom of Naples,

00:09:41.753 --> 00:09:44.133
which covered the southern half of Italy.

00:09:44.873 --> 00:09:47.673
The same king also ruled the neighbouring

00:09:47.703 --> 00:09:50.053
island of Sicily, and both kingdoms

00:09:50.053 --> 00:09:52.123
were run from his court in Naples.

00:09:53.783 --> 00:09:56.153
Sir William was in his mid-fifties,

00:09:56.513 --> 00:09:59.773
recently widowed, charming, cultured,

00:10:00.333 --> 00:10:02.543
and, most importantly, rich.

00:10:03.943 --> 00:10:06.183
He had lived in Naples for more than

00:10:06.223 --> 00:10:08.913
twenty years, and had become Europe's

00:10:08.963 --> 00:10:11.593
leading authority on volcanoes.

00:10:12.093 --> 00:10:13.633
He had even climbed Vesuvius

00:10:13.853 --> 00:10:16.053
dozens of times to peer inside.

00:10:16.633 --> 00:10:18.533
He was a collector, too, and

00:10:18.543 --> 00:10:20.033
had an extensive collection

00:10:20.203 --> 00:10:21.993
of ancient Greek vases.

00:10:23.003 --> 00:10:24.753
Crucially, from Greville's point

00:10:24.763 --> 00:10:27.763
of view, he was also childless, and

00:10:28.103 --> 00:10:30.793
Greville hoped to inherit his fortune.

00:10:31.783 --> 00:10:34.933
So Greville sat down and wrote

00:10:34.963 --> 00:10:37.533
that long, polite, carefully worded

00:10:37.533 --> 00:10:40.033
letter, proposing, in the most

00:10:40.043 --> 00:10:42.583
roundabout language possible, a deal.

00:10:43.773 --> 00:10:46.393
Sir William would take Emma off his hands.

00:10:46.743 --> 00:10:49.553
She would live in Naples as the old man's

00:10:49.553 --> 00:10:52.633
companion, and, in time, his mistress.

00:10:53.633 --> 00:10:56.213
Greville would be free to marry

00:10:56.213 --> 00:10:58.943
his rich heiress, and would stay

00:10:58.943 --> 00:11:00.783
in his rich uncle's good books.

00:11:02.059 --> 00:11:04.249
Emma was told none of this.

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:07.499
She was told she was going to Naples for a

00:11:07.499 --> 00:11:10.479
holiday, six months or so, while Greville

00:11:10.499 --> 00:11:12.859
sorted out some business up in Scotland.

00:11:14.269 --> 00:11:17.679
She arrived in April 1786, on her

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:20.039
twenty-first birthday, and settled

00:11:20.049 --> 00:11:22.149
into the ambassador's residence to

00:11:22.149 --> 00:11:24.379
wait for the man she loved, Charles

00:11:24.409 --> 00:11:26.109
Greville, to come and collect her.

00:11:27.389 --> 00:11:28.829
He never came.

00:11:29.949 --> 00:11:33.359
Slowly, over months of unanswered letters,

00:11:33.809 --> 00:11:36.199
Emma worked out what had happened.

00:11:37.059 --> 00:11:39.079
She had been passed from one

00:11:39.089 --> 00:11:41.059
man to another, like a parcel,

00:11:41.489 --> 00:11:43.353
without anyone thinking to ask her.

00:11:44.489 --> 00:11:47.069
In her letters to Greville, you can get a

00:11:47.069 --> 00:11:48.999
sense of what was going through her head.

00:11:49.579 --> 00:11:51.479
And it's not particularly surprising.

00:11:51.919 --> 00:11:55.229
She rages, she pleads, and she threatens.

00:11:56.539 --> 00:11:59.749
Greville's reply was cold and simple:

00:12:00.689 --> 00:12:03.439
be sensible, and oblige my uncle.

00:12:04.706 --> 00:12:07.896
Emma was twenty-one, alone in a foreign

00:12:07.896 --> 00:12:10.996
country, and entirely dependent on

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:13.536
yet another man she had been handed

00:12:13.536 --> 00:12:15.476
over to like a piece of property.

00:12:16.826 --> 00:12:18.886
So she made a decision.

00:12:19.866 --> 00:12:22.666
If this was the hand she had been

00:12:22.666 --> 00:12:24.806
dealt, the situation life had

00:12:24.816 --> 00:12:27.456
given her, then that was that.

00:12:27.796 --> 00:12:29.016
She would make the most out of it.

00:12:30.006 --> 00:12:31.616
She learned Italian, and then

00:12:31.616 --> 00:12:33.676
French, well enough to charm

00:12:33.696 --> 00:12:36.106
diplomats and translate at court.

00:12:36.876 --> 00:12:39.246
She trained her singing voice until

00:12:39.266 --> 00:12:41.916
professional musicians turned their heads.

00:12:43.026 --> 00:12:44.696
Sir William was a passionate

00:12:44.736 --> 00:12:46.766
collector, his house was filled with

00:12:46.816 --> 00:12:48.406
classical statues and paintings.

00:12:49.296 --> 00:12:50.986
Emma studied them all.

00:12:51.456 --> 00:12:53.506
She climbed Vesuvius with Sir William,

00:12:53.826 --> 00:12:56.856
sang for his guests, and slowly,

00:12:57.356 --> 00:12:59.426
an arrangement that had begun as a

00:12:59.456 --> 00:13:01.696
cynical transaction between two men

00:13:02.326 --> 00:13:04.606
started to look a little more equal.

00:13:05.989 --> 00:13:08.379
And out of all this, she

00:13:08.379 --> 00:13:11.239
invented something entirely new.

00:13:12.129 --> 00:13:14.949
She called them her "Attitudes".

00:13:16.619 --> 00:13:19.349
In front of an audience, wearing simple

00:13:19.359 --> 00:13:21.439
flowing dresses and using nothing

00:13:21.649 --> 00:13:23.939
but a couple of shawls, she would

00:13:23.939 --> 00:13:26.999
strike a series of poses, transforming

00:13:26.999 --> 00:13:29.109
herself from one figure of ancient

00:13:29.119 --> 00:13:31.349
history or mythology into another.

00:13:32.379 --> 00:13:34.339
One moment she was Medea,

00:13:34.509 --> 00:13:35.569
the Greek sorceress.

00:13:35.759 --> 00:13:39.009
The next, Cleopatra, then a dancing

00:13:39.049 --> 00:13:40.699
nymph, then a grieving mother,

00:13:41.039 --> 00:13:43.409
then a Neapolitan street seller.

00:13:44.759 --> 00:13:47.059
It might sound like a simple game

00:13:47.059 --> 00:13:49.759
of charades, where you act something

00:13:49.759 --> 00:13:51.989
out and everyone else has to guess.

00:13:52.409 --> 00:13:54.269
But clearly it was much

00:13:54.279 --> 00:13:55.549
more powerful than that.

00:13:56.749 --> 00:13:58.719
This was the late eighteenth century,

00:13:58.869 --> 00:14:01.619
so there are no live recordings of her

00:14:01.649 --> 00:14:03.999
performances, but we have the written

00:14:03.999 --> 00:14:06.779
accounts, and drawings of her poses

00:14:06.829 --> 00:14:08.699
that were sold as prints across Europe.

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:12.599
Audiences were clearly mesmerised.

00:14:13.489 --> 00:14:16.119
The German writer Goethe was travelling

00:14:16.119 --> 00:14:19.399
through Italy in 1787, and he

00:14:19.399 --> 00:14:20.919
watched one of these performances.

00:14:21.479 --> 00:14:23.979
He wrote that the spectator could hardly

00:14:23.979 --> 00:14:26.849
believe his eyes, that here was in

00:14:26.859 --> 00:14:29.759
living movement what the greatest artists

00:14:29.809 --> 00:14:32.289
had only managed to capture in stone.

00:14:33.411 --> 00:14:36.601
The Attitudes made Emma a celebrity across

00:14:36.601 --> 00:14:39.141
Europe, one of the sights that visitors

00:14:39.191 --> 00:14:41.991
touring the continent simply had to see.

00:14:43.321 --> 00:14:45.451
Fashionable women began copying

00:14:45.451 --> 00:14:48.211
the simple, flowing, ancient-Greek

00:14:48.211 --> 00:14:50.741
style of dress she performed in.

00:14:50.761 --> 00:14:53.121
Without meaning to, the girl from Cheshire

00:14:53.521 --> 00:14:56.281
had become one of the most influential

00:14:56.291 --> 00:14:58.741
women in European art and fashion.

00:15:00.198 --> 00:15:03.408
And Sir William, to the dismay of his

00:15:03.468 --> 00:15:06.208
aristocratic relatives, fell in love.

00:15:07.428 --> 00:15:10.098
Emma was a beautiful, younger woman,

00:15:10.258 --> 00:15:12.718
that much is clear, but she was

00:15:12.938 --> 00:15:16.058
intelligent, great company, and she

00:15:16.058 --> 00:15:18.058
began to share in his interests.

00:15:19.258 --> 00:15:21.288
It wasn't a one-way thing,

00:15:21.288 --> 00:15:23.338
though; the feeling was mutual.

00:15:24.508 --> 00:15:27.048
It had been an arranged relationship,

00:15:27.398 --> 00:15:29.488
and there was a thirty-four-year

00:15:29.558 --> 00:15:31.738
age gap between the pair, but

00:15:32.128 --> 00:15:34.268
they got on fabulously, and

00:15:34.498 --> 00:15:36.288
she became deeply fond of him.

00:15:37.798 --> 00:15:41.238
In 1791, during a visit to London,

00:15:41.748 --> 00:15:43.448
the sixty-year-old ambassador

00:15:43.848 --> 00:15:45.208
and the twenty-six-year-old

00:15:45.218 --> 00:15:47.268
blacksmith's daughter were married.

00:15:48.518 --> 00:15:51.828
Amy Lyon, then Emma Hart, was now

00:15:52.218 --> 00:15:55.008
Lady Hamilton, wife of the British

00:15:55.038 --> 00:15:57.398
ambassador to the Kingdom of Naples.

00:15:58.268 --> 00:16:00.388
The girl who had scrubbed floors

00:16:00.598 --> 00:16:02.558
as a twelve-year-old maid, the girl

00:16:02.798 --> 00:16:05.018
who had quite possibly sold herself

00:16:05.098 --> 00:16:06.918
on the streets of London, she was

00:16:06.918 --> 00:16:09.458
now the hostess of one of the most

00:16:09.488 --> 00:16:12.078
glamorous diplomatic houses in Europe.

00:16:13.458 --> 00:16:15.688
And she was exceptionally good

00:16:15.688 --> 00:16:18.393
at it; even the snobs who sneered

00:16:18.393 --> 00:16:20.838
at her background admitted that

00:16:20.838 --> 00:16:22.728
she ran the embassy brilliantly.

00:16:23.818 --> 00:16:25.508
One thing she never lost,

00:16:25.508 --> 00:16:27.018
though, was her accent.

00:16:27.608 --> 00:16:29.968
Emma spoke English with the strong

00:16:29.988 --> 00:16:32.118
accent of the northwest of England.

00:16:32.768 --> 00:16:34.958
In Naples, it simply didn't matter

00:16:34.958 --> 00:16:37.278
though; she charmed the aristocracy of

00:16:37.278 --> 00:16:40.309
Europe in Italian and French, where her

00:16:40.309 --> 00:16:42.798
accent simply sounded "foreign", and

00:16:42.998 --> 00:16:45.358
she was somewhat immune to the snobbery

00:16:45.368 --> 00:16:47.298
that would have existed had she been

00:16:47.298 --> 00:16:49.658
speaking English with English aristocrats.

00:16:51.208 --> 00:16:54.068
More surprising still, she became the

00:16:54.098 --> 00:16:57.298
best friend and closest confidante of

00:16:57.368 --> 00:16:59.718
Maria Carolina, the Queen of Naples.

00:17:01.088 --> 00:17:02.948
The two women saw each other

00:17:03.018 --> 00:17:05.058
constantly and wrote to each other

00:17:05.278 --> 00:17:07.038
sometimes several times a day.

00:17:08.368 --> 00:17:11.578
And Maria Carolina held real power.

00:17:12.498 --> 00:17:15.528
Her husband, King Ferdinand, was a bit

00:17:15.528 --> 00:17:18.088
of a fool, and preferred hunting and

00:17:18.088 --> 00:17:20.088
fishing to the business of government.

00:17:20.868 --> 00:17:23.028
Most of that he left to his wife.

00:17:23.548 --> 00:17:26.298
She sat on the royal council, and in

00:17:26.298 --> 00:17:28.558
practice she ran much of the kingdom.

00:17:29.778 --> 00:17:31.618
Now, business between two

00:17:31.618 --> 00:17:34.378
countries normally flowed from

00:17:34.378 --> 00:17:36.298
king and ministers to ambassador.

00:17:37.358 --> 00:17:40.928
In Naples, much of it now flowed through

00:17:40.928 --> 00:17:43.128
this friendship between two women,

00:17:43.668 --> 00:17:46.548
Maria Carolina and Lady Hamilton.

00:17:48.178 --> 00:17:50.208
By any measure of her world,

00:17:50.578 --> 00:17:52.598
Emma had reached the top.

00:17:53.865 --> 00:17:55.265
Meanwhile, there was a

00:17:55.275 --> 00:17:56.975
revolution going on in France.

00:17:57.555 --> 00:18:01.015
In 1792, the revolutionaries threw

00:18:01.035 --> 00:18:02.975
the French royal family into prison,

00:18:03.315 --> 00:18:06.065
including Marie Antoinette, who just so

00:18:06.065 --> 00:18:09.025
happened to be Maria Carolina's sister.

00:18:10.438 --> 00:18:12.778
To state the obvious, Maria Carolina

00:18:12.948 --> 00:18:14.858
had little time for France and its

00:18:14.858 --> 00:18:17.588
revolution, and this would become the

00:18:17.598 --> 00:18:20.168
driving force of Neapolitan politics.

00:18:21.488 --> 00:18:25.468
By 1793, the revolutionaries had executed

00:18:25.508 --> 00:18:28.588
Louis XVI, and France was at war with

00:18:28.608 --> 00:18:30.702
Britain and with most of its neighbours.

00:18:31.548 --> 00:18:36.338
In September 1793, a short, slight,

00:18:36.608 --> 00:18:39.038
ambitious young navy captain sailed

00:18:39.068 --> 00:18:41.038
into the Bay of Naples, asking

00:18:41.228 --> 00:18:43.418
for Neapolitan soldiers to help

00:18:43.558 --> 00:18:45.088
in the fight against the French.

00:18:46.038 --> 00:18:48.208
His name was Horatio Nelson.

00:18:49.614 --> 00:18:51.874
On that first visit, nothing

00:18:51.874 --> 00:18:53.754
of particular note happened.

00:18:54.144 --> 00:18:56.924
He stayed for a few days, was entertained

00:18:56.924 --> 00:18:59.264
at the embassy, and sailed away again.

00:19:00.634 --> 00:19:02.594
Five years later, in the summer

00:19:02.864 --> 00:19:06.144
of 1798, Nelson was back in the

00:19:06.144 --> 00:19:08.714
Mediterranean, hunting Napoleon's fleet.

00:19:10.104 --> 00:19:12.784
We touched on this in the last episode,

00:19:13.054 --> 00:19:15.624
but let me give you the full story.

00:19:16.864 --> 00:19:18.874
Nelson's ships were running

00:19:18.884 --> 00:19:21.644
dangerously low on food and water.

00:19:22.654 --> 00:19:24.234
He needed a friendly port,

00:19:24.464 --> 00:19:26.474
somewhere to get fresh supplies.

00:19:27.174 --> 00:19:29.984
The obvious place was Sicily, which

00:19:29.984 --> 00:19:31.844
was ruled by the King of Naples.

00:19:32.334 --> 00:19:35.204
But there was a problem: Naples

00:19:35.464 --> 00:19:38.174
was officially neutral, and a

00:19:38.174 --> 00:19:40.614
neutral kingdom could not openly

00:19:40.854 --> 00:19:42.674
supply a British war fleet.

00:19:44.244 --> 00:19:46.664
Emma went to the Queen, Maria

00:19:46.664 --> 00:19:48.964
Carolina, and asked her to help.

00:19:50.387 --> 00:19:52.577
We don't know exactly what was said.

00:19:52.997 --> 00:19:55.427
But we know the Queen trusted Emma

00:19:55.477 --> 00:19:58.447
completely, and we know she was willing

00:19:58.447 --> 00:20:00.817
to take risks for her friend that she

00:20:00.817 --> 00:20:03.197
might not have taken for an ambassador.

00:20:04.427 --> 00:20:06.357
Exactly how much difference her

00:20:06.387 --> 00:20:08.947
influence made is still argued over by

00:20:08.947 --> 00:20:12.067
historians, but the outcome is clear.

00:20:12.907 --> 00:20:14.787
Orders went out to Sicily,

00:20:15.147 --> 00:20:17.367
Nelson's ships were allowed to

00:20:17.377 --> 00:20:19.567
dock at the port of Syracuse.

00:20:19.987 --> 00:20:21.667
They were given everything they

00:20:21.667 --> 00:20:23.867
needed, and the hunt continued.

00:20:25.497 --> 00:20:27.927
A week later, Nelson found the

00:20:27.927 --> 00:20:29.647
French fleet anchored at the

00:20:29.647 --> 00:20:31.967
mouth of the Nile, in Egypt, and

00:20:31.977 --> 00:20:34.087
destroyed it almost completely.

00:20:34.567 --> 00:20:36.497
It was one of the greatest naval

00:20:36.507 --> 00:20:39.237
victories in history, and it left

00:20:39.297 --> 00:20:41.767
Napoleon's army completely cut off.

00:20:42.807 --> 00:20:44.837
Nelson believed that without

00:20:44.897 --> 00:20:46.847
Emma's assistance, this

00:20:47.117 --> 00:20:48.367
would not have been possible.

00:20:49.677 --> 00:20:54.087
So, in September 1798, the most celebrated

00:20:54.087 --> 00:20:56.807
man in Europe sailed back into the

00:20:56.807 --> 00:20:59.997
Bay of Naples, wounded, exhausted,

00:21:00.357 --> 00:21:02.147
and looking for somewhere to recover.

00:21:03.387 --> 00:21:05.787
Waiting for him on the quayside, at the

00:21:05.787 --> 00:21:08.477
head of the biggest welcome party Naples

00:21:08.697 --> 00:21:11.657
had ever thrown, was Emma Hamilton.

00:21:12.627 --> 00:21:14.667
The blacksmith's daughter and the

00:21:14.667 --> 00:21:17.077
parson's son were about to collide.

00:21:17.897 --> 00:21:19.407
And that is what we will

00:21:19.407 --> 00:21:20.857
leave for part three.

00:21:22.277 --> 00:21:24.317
OK, then, that is it for part

00:21:24.317 --> 00:21:26.137
two of our three-part mini-series

00:21:26.307 --> 00:21:27.967
on Nelson and Lady Hamilton.

00:21:28.247 --> 00:21:30.737
Next up, in part three, it's the love

00:21:30.767 --> 00:21:33.707
affair itself: the strange three-sided

00:21:33.707 --> 00:21:35.837
household, the scandal that gripped

00:21:35.857 --> 00:21:38.327
Britain, Trafalgar, and the dying

00:21:38.327 --> 00:21:40.857
wish that Britain chose to ignore.

00:21:41.627 --> 00:21:43.547
You've been listening to English Learning

00:21:43.547 --> 00:21:46.327
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:21:46.627 --> 00:21:49.557
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:21:49.557 --> 00:21:51.537
I'll catch you in the next episode.

